window.docContents[5].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Radial-Symmetry-by-Moving-Planes":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity","header":"5.3.5. Radial Symmetry by Moving Planes","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Radial-Symmetry-by-Moving-Planes"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-foundations":{"contents":"Formalized: completed finite-exponent integer-order graph spaces,\nmollification and density, first-order extension on operational \ndomains, finite-exponent trace operators, Hilbert quotient trace spaces and\ntheir minimal-energy extensions on flat and operational  domains,\ncanonical agreement of the finite-exponent and Hilbert trace theories at\n, zero-boundary spaces, Poincaré, Hardy, Sobolev and Morrey inequalities, the\nfull strict subcritical Rellich–Kondrachov range, weak product and pullback\ncalculus, and the integer-order comparison with Fourier-defined Sobolev\nspaces. On bounded operational  domains, arbitrary-offset\none-Lipschitz  scalar maps with derivative bounded by one define\ncompleted finite-exponent Nemytskii operations and satisfy the weak chain\nrule. Arbitrary ordered interval truncations are completed operations on\nthese bounded domains and satisfy the sharp open-interval derivative formula.\nFor every , every zero-preserving real Lipschitz map acts\non  and satisfies the full almost-everywhere weak\nchain rule with its classical derivative. On bounded operational \ndomains, every real Lipschitz scalar map acts on  without\na zero-preserving hypothesis and satisfies the same chain rule.\n\nHigher-order bounded extension is also formalized for every positive integer\n on bounded open domains with  graph boundary, for every\n. The construction from only an operational \natlas remains first order. The global one-dimensional  theory has\nan absolutely continuous representative, almost-everywhere classical\nderivative, and finite-kink calculus for absolute value, positive and negative\nparts, and interval truncations. In every positive dimension, each global\n class has an explicit absolutely continuous representative on\nalmost every line parallel to every coordinate axis, with the corresponding\nweak derivative as its classical derivative almost everywhere.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.4. Sobolev foundations","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-foundations"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Distributional-support":{"contents":"A distribution-like functional vanishes on a set  when it annihilates\nevery test function whose topological support lies in . The definition\nallows arbitrary . The complement of its support is the union of all\nopen vanishing sets; equivalently, distributional support is the\nintersection of all closed sets whose complements are vanishing sets:Mathlib's definition is abstract enough to apply to both classical and\ntempered distributions. The probe space is the one present in the functional\nitself: ordinary distributions are tested by , while tempered\ndistributions are tested by .\n\nClosedness is the property that lets support arguments pass to complements and\nwork with open vanishing sets.\n\nThe distributional support of every distribution is closed:\n\nEvery set appearing in the defining intersection is closed, and an arbitrary\nintersection of closed sets is closed.\n\nThe derivative support law is the tempered counterpart of the statement for\nordinary distributions, and the one-term case of the constant-coefficient\nsupport theorem.\n\nFor  with  a complex normed space,\ndistributional differentiation cannot enlarge support:\n\nIf  vanishes on an open set, then it annihilates the derivative of every\ntest function supported there because differentiation preserves the support\ninclusion. The derivative distribution therefore vanishes on every open set\nwhere  vanishes, which gives the support inclusion.\n\nThe Dirac mass is the test case in which the support is computed exactly rather\nthan bounded. The inclusion in  uses only vanishing away from ; the\nreverse inclusion needs a Schwartz function not vanishing at  with support\ninside an arbitrary neighborhood, which is where finite-dimensionality of \nis used.\n\nIn finite dimensions the support of the tempered Dirac distribution at  is exactly the\nsingleton :\n\nThe Dirac mass vanishes on the complement of , so its support is\ncontained in that singleton. Every neighborhood of  contains a smooth\ncompactly supported function that is nonzero at ; evaluating the Dirac\nmass on this function proves the reverse inclusion.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.5. Distributional support","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Distributional-support"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Mathematical-Transition-at-Shock-Formation":{"contents":"Characteristics solve a conservation law right up until the characteristic map\nstops being invertible. Once characteristics intersect, classical derivatives are\ngone and the equation has to be read through an integral identity instead.\nFor Burgers, that identity admits both compression and expansion shocks. An entropy\ncondition distinguishes them and supplies the additional selection needed\nfor scalar uniqueness.\n\nFor scalar equations we follow that transition; for finite-dimensional systems\nwe build the parallel local geometry. Burgers's equation and the p-system are\nthe concrete models, since shocks, rarefactions, entropy, and initial traces\ncan all be checked explicitly in them.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.2.1. The Mathematical Transition at Shock Formation","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Mathematical-Transition-at-Shock-Formation"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives":{"contents":"A bound on the Dirichlet energy controls the gradient in , but does not\nensure classical differentiability or pointwise boundary values. In existence\nproofs, weak derivatives let us retain derivative bounds under weak convergence.\nCompactness and trace theorems then supply stronger convergence and boundary\nvalues under additional hypotheses.\n\nFor locally integrable functions  and , the weak-derivative identity is\n for every smooth\ncompactly supported  in . Requiring the function and its derivatives\nthrough order  to belong to  gives , equipped with\na graph norm.\n\nTwo useful consequences are compactness and continuity. On a domain with the\nbounded  boundary atlas used by the extension theorem, the\nRellich–Kondrachov theorem makes\nthe embedding  compact for\n and . Bounded sequences therefore have subsequences\nconverging strongly in those  spaces; evolution arguments also need\ncontrol in time to obtain strong spacetime convergence.\nThe Morrey embedding\nsends  into Hölder-continuous functions for .\nApplied to sufficiently many weak derivatives, it recovers classical regularity\nfrom the elliptic Sobolev estimates.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"4. Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives"}});